fontselect: Android's fonts for the letters no other font has

libass knows three ways of asking a system for fonts, Core Text, fontconfig
and DirectWrite, and Android has none of them, so a build for Android had
no font provider at all. A host could hand it font files and name a family
and a file to fall back on, but a letter none of those has was drawn as an
empty box: libass looks for a letter in another font only by asking the
default provider (get_fallback), and the fonts handed over are found by the
family names a style gives and by nothing else. A subtitle in Arabic,
Hebrew, Thai or a script of India, under a style that names Arial, came
out as boxes.

This adds a provider of Android's (libass/ass_android.c, "android",
ASS_FONTPROVIDER_ANDROID, chosen by ASS_FONTPROVIDER_AUTODETECT). Its one
callback of substance is get_fallback: it asks the NDK's AFontMatcher
(Android 10, API 29) which font Android's own text stack draws the letter
in, checks that the font has it, lists the font with libass under a family
name of its own, and answers with that name; libass then opens the font by
its path, as it opens any font file. The bold of that font is listed under
the same name where the system has a separate file for it. Letters are
remembered with their answer, since libass asks again for a letter no font
has each time a line holding it is laid out.

It never matches a style's family name against the system's fonts: that
would mean opening every font the system has, and it would change what the
host chose for a line whose font nobody has. For the same reason the
default font (ass_set_fonts' default path), which on Android is the font
the host chose for the reader's script, keeps every letter it has: in
ass_font_select, the provider is asked only when that font lacks the
letter. So a line looks as it did with no provider at all, less the boxes.

Two limits of libass's own go with it. A font of a line's style held ten
faces at most, for the whole track, and with fonts found a letter at a time
every script a track uses takes one: a track in nine scripts spent all ten,
and its Mongolian and Cherokee were boxes. It holds 32 now. And the default
font was given no id when it was chosen, so a font that had it as a face
already opened another copy of it for every letter no font has, until it
had no room left for a font that has a letter; it has one id now.

The provider is compiled by the build script on its own (Makefile.in does
not know the file) and libass is linked with -landroid.

